---
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import { specs, versions } from '../data/facts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Vision & Roadmap"
  description="The long-term research direction for RoboLang and the status of each stage: what is delivered, what runs in simulation only, and what is planned. Nothing on this page runs on a robot."
>
  <PageHead slot="head" index="05" label="Vision & Roadmap" title="Vision & roadmap">
    Where RoboLang is trying to go, and how that differs from what you can install today.
  </PageHead>

  <div class="prose">
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        <strong>Plans and features are marked apart.</strong> Stages marked Delivered or Simulation only
        are in the core repository today. Nothing on this page runs on a robot, enforces a safety
        limit or includes an AI model, and plans may change.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Vision</h2>
    <p>
      The research question behind RoboLang is whether one small, precisely specified task language
      can sit between the people and AI systems that decide what a robot should do and the varied
      software that makes robots move.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Interoperable tasks.</strong> The same task description, checked once, understood by
        different robots through adapters that declare what each robot can do.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multi-robot communication.</strong> Structured, versioned messages for handing work
        from one robot to another, in place of free text.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI-generated programs.</strong> A language strict enough that a task written by a
        language model can be rejected with a precise reason before it reaches anything physical.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The project builds toward this in order: a correct language first, then execution in simulation,
      then interoperability. Each stage is meant to be verifiable before the next begins.
    </p>

    <h2>Stages</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Stage</th>
            <th scope="col">Focus</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.1 (RL-11)</th>
            <td>
              Language correctness: specification and grammar, parser, static validation, versioned IR,
              the <code>validate</code> and <code>inspect</code> commands and an installable package.
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong> as distribution {versions.distribution}
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Foundation 1 (RL-BASIC-01)</th>
            <td>
              Simulation-only execution: an executor with explicit states, preconditions evaluated
              against a declared world file, fail-closed behavior and a deterministic run record
              (<code>robolang simulate</code>).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Delivered</strong>, simulation only. Signed off 2026-10-27
              (<a href={specs.foundation1Evidence}>evidence</a>)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Language 1.2 (LANG-1)</th>
            <td>
              Conditions combined with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and <code>not</code>, and IR
              schema 1.1. A language version, separate from the milestones in this table.
            </td>
            <td><strong>Delivered</strong></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Gate 1 (RL-ARCH-02)</th>
            <td>
              Core {versions.core} message and capability contracts, and an in-process reference
              simulation in which an AI planner stub and two robot stubs exchange A2R and R2R messages
              (<code>robolang arch02</code>).
            </td>
            <td>
              <strong>Simulation only.</strong> Gate 1 passed, signed off 2026-10-27
              (<a href={specs.gate1Evidence}>evidence</a>)
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Gate 2 (RL-INTEROP-01)</th>
            <td>
              Independent interoperability: a participant built in a different codebase, from the
              published documents alone, works with the reference implementation.
            </td>
            <td>
              Specification approved 2026-10-27 (<a href={specs.gate2}>gate 2 specification</a>).
              Implementation not started and not yet authorized
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Foundation 2</th>
            <td>
              Intelligent behavior: goal execution, observations, a knowledge store, reasoner
              proposals, bounded recovery and a deeper world model.
            </td>
            <td>Planned. Not started</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Physical robots</th>
            <td>Running tasks on real hardware, through adapters and local safety controls.</td>
            <td>Not started and not authorized</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Milestones are named by their work packages. Language versions (1.1, 1.2) name the syntax of
      <code>.rob</code> files and are separate from the milestones. An earlier version of this plan
      used "v1.2" for the execution milestone; that milestone is Foundation 1 above and is unrelated
      to language 1.2. All of these are separate from the distribution version of the Python package;
      see <a href="/get-started/#versions">the version numbers</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The full plan, with the scope and exit criteria for each stage, is the
      <a href={specs.developmentPlan}>development plan</a> in the core repository. The architecture
      the stages build toward is <a href={specs.rfc001}>RFC-001</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Physical robots</h2>
    <p>
      Running RoboLang against real hardware is outside the current scope of the project. The supported
      package cannot do it: its simulations are symbolic and run in one process. The older prototype
      code in the repository has never been validated against ROS 2 or a robot. Please do not connect
      RoboLang to physical equipment.
    </p>

    <h2>Help shape it</h2>
    <p>
      The most useful input at this stage is on the language and the contracts: what is awkward to
      express, which diagnostics are unclear, what a consumer of the JSON IR or a run record needs, and
      where Core {versions.core} is ambiguous. See
      <a href="/contribute/">Contribute</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
